proof=r'''## Proof

**Given:** The Statement, full AC, and the exact analytic supplier Statements [A1], [A3]–[A5]; their unfinished authoring remains the hold in [L1]. We prove the absolute assertion by induction through dimensions $0,1,2,3$, then prove the stated family comparison.

1.1 At a complex point, coherent algebraic and analytic modules are finite-dimensional complex vector spaces, so the absolute assertions hold in dimension zero. Flatness in [A1] makes analytification exact stalkwise, including for nonreduced finite-type schemes. Tensor associativity gives tensor compatibility and compatibility with quotient restriction to a hyperplane. A finite algebraic presentation analytifies to a finite analytic presentation, so [A3] ensures its cokernel is coherent. For a closed analytic embedding, direct image is exact on stalks and preserves flabby sheaves: its sections over an open are the original sections over the intersection with the closed subspace. Pushing forward a flabby resolution therefore identifies cohomology on the closed subspace and its direct image. These facts apply to the hyperplane sequences below. [A1, A3, F5, algebra]

2.1 Suppose the absolute theorem has been proved in dimensions below $r$, where $1\le r\le3$. By [A5] and [F3], cohomology comparison holds for $\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^r}$. The exact hyperplane sequences $0\to\mathcal O(m-1)\to\mathcal O(m)\to i_*\mathcal O_H(m)\to0$ exist on both sides by step 1.1. The comparison for $H\cong\mathbb P^{r-1}$, the long exact sequences and [F4] propagate comparison from $m=0$ to every positive and negative integer $m$. In degree zero this is the natural section map, so holomorphic sections of every twisting line bundle are exactly its algebraic sections. [A1, A5, F3, F4, step 1.1, induction]

3.1 Resolve an algebraic coherent $\mathcal F$ by [F2]. Its finite twisting-sheaf resolution stays exact analytically by step 1.1. Starting at its leftmost term, the long exact sequences and step 2.1 prove cohomology comparison for each successive cokernel and hence for $\mathcal F$ in every degree. This uses the actual finite resolution, not an unproved global analytic free resolution. [A1, F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, induction]

4.1 For finitely presented algebraic $\mathcal F$, a finite presentation computes the sheaf $\mathcal H om(\mathcal F,\mathcal G)$ as a kernel between finite powers of $\mathcal G$. Exact analytification and [A3] therefore identify its analytification with analytic $\mathcal H om(\mathcal F^{an},\mathcal G^{an})$. Step 3.1 in degree zero identifies morphisms. A finite locally free resolution of $\mathcal F$ computes global $\operatorname{Ext}^q$ by the hypercohomology of its Hom complex: locally free Hom is exact, and an injective resolution gives the resulting double-complex comparison. Termwise cohomology comparison and finiteness of the resolution identify algebraic and analytic Ext in every degree. Thus full faithfulness and cohomology/Ext comparison in dimension $r$ are proved before essential surjectivity. [A1, A3, F2, F5, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 Let $\mathcal M$ now be any coherent analytic sheaf on $\mathbb P^r$. For every nonzero linear section $s$, retain both $K_s=\ker(\mathcal M(-1)\xrightarrow{s}\mathcal M)$ and $Q_s=\operatorname{coker}(s)$, and write $I_s=\operatorname{im}(s)$. Locally $s$ is a hyperplane equation, so it annihilates $K_s$ and $Q_s$; [A3] makes them coherent sheaves on $H_s\cong\mathbb P^{r-1}$. The inductive equivalence algebraizes them. By [F3], their positive cohomology vanishes after sufficiently positive twists. The two exact sequences $0\to K_s(n)\to\mathcal M(n-1)\to I_s(n)\to0$ and $0\to I_s(n)\to\mathcal M(n)\to Q_s(n)\to0$ then show that multiplication by $s$ on $H^q(\mathcal M(n-1))$ is eventually an isomorphism for $q\ge2$, and eventually surjective for $q=1$. The $H^0(Q_s(n))$ term prevents an initial claim of injectivity in degree one. This argument includes hyperplane torsion; $s$ was not assumed injective on $\mathcal M$. [A3, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, induction]

5.2 Fix $q>0$ and a nonzero $s_0$. For $q=1$, the eventual surjections in step 5.1 make the finite dimensions in [A4] nonincreasing integers, hence eventually constant; thereafter multiplication by $s_0$ is an isomorphism. For $q\ge2$ it is already eventually an isomorphism. Choose $N$ beyond this range, put $V=H^q(\mathcal M(N))$, and identify each $H^q(\mathcal M(n))$, $n\ge N$, with $V$ by successive multiplication by $s_0$. A second independent section $s_1$ induces an endomorphism $T_n$ on $V$ between successive degrees. The commuting multiplication square gives $T_{n+1}=T_n=T$. If $V\ne0$, choose an eigenvalue $\lambda$ by [F7]. Multiplication by the nonzero section $s_1-\lambda s_0$ then induces $T-\lambda I$, singular at every such degree. But step 5.1 applied to this fixed section is eventually an isomorphism for $q\ge2$; for $q=1$ it is eventually surjective between spaces of the same stabilized dimension and hence an isomorphism too. This contradiction proves eventual vanishing. The eigenvalue is chosen before its section's threshold; no bound uniform over an infinite pencil is used. [A4, F7, step 5.1, algebra]

5.3 A simultaneous bound is available. For $1\le q\le r$ take the maximum of the finitely many bounds from step 5.2. If $q>r$, the inductive algebraic dimension bound [F7] makes all relevant cohomology groups of $K_s,Q_s$ in step 5.1 zero at every twist. Multiplication by every nonzero $s$ is then an isomorphism on $H^q(\mathcal M(n))$ at every integer $n$. The same two-section contradiction starting at any degree forces these groups to be zero at every twist. Thus one integer bounds vanishing for all $q>0$. [F7, step 5.1, step 5.2, algebra]

5.4 Fix a point $p$ and choose a hyperplane $H_s$ through it. By the inductive equivalence, $Q_s$ is algebraic. Its finite resolution [F2] begins with a surjection from a finite sum of twisting sheaves. After a sufficiently positive twist each summand is generated by its finite homogeneous monomials, so $Q_s(n)$ has finitely many global generators, analytically as well by inductive comparison. Also choose $n$ so $H^1(\mathcal M(n-1))=0$ by step 5.3 and $H^2(K_s(n))=0$ by induction and [F3]. The first exact sequence in step 5.1 gives $H^1(I_s(n))=0$; the second then lifts those finitely many generators to global sections of $\mathcal M(n)$. The cokernel $C$ of these lifts satisfies $C_p/sC_p=0$, so local Nakayama [F7] gives $C_p=0$. Finite local generation of the coherent cokernel gives an open neighborhood of $p$ on which the lifts generate. [A3, F2, F3, F7, step 5.1, step 5.3, construct]

5.5 Shrink each such neighborhood into a coordinate nonvanishing set $\{\ell_p\ne0\}$, with generating sections of some twist $n_p$. Compactness gives finitely many neighborhoods $U_j$ covering projective space, with integers $n_j$. For every $n\ge\max_j n_j$, multiply the $j$th generating set by $\ell_j^{n-n_j}$. These are global sections of $\mathcal M(n)$ and still generate on $U_j$, since $\ell_j$ is a unit there. Their finite union generates everywhere. This proves analytic positive-twist generation from the exact earlier suppliers. No assertion about finite pole order of arbitrary entire functions on an affine chart is used. [step 5.4, construct]

6.1 Apply step 5.5 to $\mathcal M$ to obtain a surjection $\mathcal O(-a)^u\to\mathcal M$. Its kernel is coherent by [A3], so a second application gives a finite presentation of $\mathcal M$ by sums of twisting sheaves. By step 2.1 every matrix entry is an algebraic section of the corresponding twisting line bundle. Take its algebraic cokernel; step 1.1 identifies its analytification with $\mathcal M$. Step 4.1 algebraizes each isomorphism and its inverse and preserves their identity composites. This proves essential surjectivity and completes the absolute induction through dimensions $0,1,2,3$, including the claimed dimensions two and three. [A1, A3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.1, step 5.5, construct]

'''
